All three of the countries I follow are burning in a very specific way. South Africa has declared femicide—the intentional killing of women with a gender-related motivation—a national emergency. Lesotho's crisis goes largely undocumented. And the US, which accounts for the [majority of femicides](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11991417/) among countries on its income level, has yet to legally define or systematically track it. Every day, [137 women and girls are killed](https://www.unwomen.org/en/news-stories/press-release/2025/11/every-day-137-women-and-girls-are-killed-by-intimate-partners-or-family-members) by intimate partners or family members globally, with men perpetrating the most violence. It is one of the most devastating manifestations of teaching boys that they are superior and owed obedience by girls. And yet, these same harmful messages also wound boys themselves.\
\
This past May marked International Day of the Boy Child, a day the United Nations has yet to officially recognize. A UN day doesn't change everything, but after twenty years in international development in my other life, I know that recognition at that level amplifies attention, and attention brings resources, and resources bring insight, and insight, eventually, brings change. Boys didn't choose patriarchy. Like girls, they are born into it. We need to change what we tell boys about masculinity and gender equality—not just for women and girls' sake, but for theirs too.\

**The Tragedy of Inheriting Harmful Lessons**

Years ago, when I interviewed men convicted of killing their intimate partners for my thesis research, I found something astonishing. Even from prison, all of them justified their violence through the social norms they'd learned as boys. The messages around women's obligation to comply and care for them had become identity, persisting even with their wives dead and their own lives interrupted. One man told me, “Before, she used to be a good wife, she cooked and cleaned.” For a man to fear losing control over his partner more than he fears his own violence should tell us much about unhealthy masculinity or [the man box](https://www.acalltomen.org/resources/video-what-is-the-man-box/)—the rigid socialization of boys about what they are supposed to be and do.

**Privilege? Please!**

In Lesotho, where I am from, girls consistently outperform boys academically, and women achieve higher literacy rates with a gap of over 13 percentage points. I will never forget the first child herder I met in Semonkong, Maseru, during an early career role with a local NGO. He was under 10, herding sheep, and in shock, I started up a conversation. He nonchalantly narrated that the family had decided he would leave school to work. It was the norm, my older colleagues said. That is part of the reason for the literacy gap. And yet, the statistics are compounded by the cultural messages that tell boys they inherit superiority and authority at birth, leaving them with less incentive to earn competence. I think of that boy, and all the ways that statement is wrong, whenever I hear that gender equality would only mean boys and men losing ‘privilege.’ In reality, the man box is a form of cruelty to the boy, masked as privilege.

This isn’t just theoretical. In Lesotho, [men die by suicide](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9476842/) at over three times the rate of women. Men in South Africa and the US are also more likely to die by suicide than women. In South Africa, the refrain [*indoda* must](https://www.sowetan.co.za/s-mag/wellness/2022-06-06-love-language-mastering-girlfriend-allowance/)—a man must—creates merciless pressure for men, as does the concept of providing a "girlfriend allowance." In its own way, that pressure applies in the US, and not meeting that standard elicits shame and defeat for many men. Sixty percent of US men who die by suicide are reportedly experiencing situational crises such as job loss, divorce, or financial stress, crises that clash with their distorted masculine identity. \
\
Of course, boys inherit structural advantages that girls are denied, such as access to power, freedom of movement, and economic opportunity. It is an undeniable robbery of girls’ realities and futures. But boys did not choose patriarchy. They, too, are born and marinated from infancy in it. We cannot continue to overlook the accompanying emotional costs that place boys in a toxic masculinity cage—ways of being casually transmitted from primary caregivers all the way to school environments.

Imagine being “privileged” but with a sense of worth tied only to your bank balance, control over others, or your position in relation to women. Privileged, but denied deep, emotionally intimate friendships. Privileged, but unable to enter and enjoy the benefits of a full partnership with an equal. Privileged, but denied the opportunity to nurture your own children directly. Privileged, but restricted from exploring your interests freely. What kind of "privilege" denies you access to emotional literacy, rendering you unable to process grief or fear without destruction of self or others? What privilege tells a child: *monna ke nku ha a lle*, boys don't cry, *indoda ayikhali*, barring the boy from releasing stress, expressing pain, or asking for help without shame?

**How’s Compliance Over Aspiration Working For Us?**

Only a small percentage of [gender equality efforts](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6747894/) engage boys and men, and it comes as no surprise that the latest Equimundo report reveals that most boys say they feel like [no one really knows them](https://www.equimundo.org/resources/state-of-american-men/). Instead of finding out who our boys are and have the capacity to become as individuals, and encouraging them to do the same, social and the few programmatic messages are [largely compliance-based](https://www.unwomen.org/en/digital-library/publications/2021/11/policy-brief-working-with-men-and-boys-for-gender-equality?utm_source=chatgpt.com): Don't hit women. Don't rape. Don't abuse. Be an ally. End violence. Treating violence prevention as the main goal of gender equality implicitly tells boys that they are predisposed to and hold some predetermined power to do these things. \
\
By implying that boys are inherently inclined to violate and must therefore choose restraint, we scar boys and reinforce toxic masculinity’s idea that boys’ relationship to girls is one of power over. Instead of transforming harmful norms, we are asking boys to be "charitable" within the same structure we need to be advocating against. We now know that the current gap in messaging and support is partly filled by the [manosphere](https://www.unwomen.org/en/articles/explainer/what-is-the-manosphere-and-why-should-we-care), loose communities that [target boys and men](https://uk.movember.com/movember-institute/masculinities-report) with [often harmful advice](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DY4Vsi7isTD/?igsh=MTBtdWdrNmltMHo0dA==).&nbsp; \
\

**Building a Different Legacy for Boys**

No boy is born dreaming of a legacy of limitation, dominance, or harm. Our responsibility is to help the boy in our lives grow into the legacy he would choose if society didn't teach him otherwise. Legacy-focused messaging moves from prevention to promotion, building toward[ excellence, wholeness, and authentic selfhood](https://donmcpherson.com/aspirationalmasculinity). Legacy-focused messaging tells boys early on that they, too, are beautiful and worthy just by being born. What we know now about what many men suffer and perpetrate asks of us to tell boys explicitly that they are not defined by what they can do to, with, or for girls and women. Experts remind us that as we raise boys, we need to emphasize that [they are not bad](https://www.cnn.com/2022/06/10/health/masculinity-conversation-boys-wellness/index.html) simply because they are male. \
\
But we can't just tell boys things—we have to show them, which has better hope for transforming norms. Boys need to know the limitless potential of what they can be and do. Exposing them to men who are rejecting gendered lives is a good start: fathers doing dishes, caring, men asking for help, crying, collaborating, and making decisions with their partners. They need to see men not just working without shame but thriving in female-dominated professional fields. It is the difference between a legacy of limitation and a legacy of limitless potential—and too often, the difference between life and death.

**We Should All Shed the Role of Patriarchy’s Helpers**

Patriarchy has successfully outsourced its dangerous work to mothers, fathers, authority figures, and communities, so that the system runs effectively beyond what men themselves do and don't do. We must work to be acutely aware and [interrupt, on a daily basis](https://www.equimundo.org/resources/9-tips-for-parents-raising-sons-to-embrace-healthy-positive-masculinity/), the small and big ways we do that work—how we consciously and subconsciously amplify its girl-demeaning and boy-limiting messages and norms.\
\

As support for gender equality dwindles, individuals, families, and communities all need to identify and use the power we have to reject the role of perpetuating gender inequality. Before a man becomes someone we call a monster, he was a boy shaped by what we said, what we showed, and what we allowed. To raise different men, we must change the script. The message, both preached and lived, must be that boys are beautiful; that they are not made for dominance, but for dignity, for the pursuit of a worthy legacy, for care of themselves and others, and for mutual respect. The returns on this informed, collective action will drive us closer to gender equality and the well-being of girls, boys, women, men, and those of other gender identities than what we have managed so far.
